10:22:44 | INFO | 🤖 Target: A thermostat setting of 65°F means “try to maintain the cabin air around 65°F,” not “your body will experience the exact same thermal conditions.” Even if the air truly is 65°F in both cases, AC changes two major things: - It **dehumidifies** the air. - It usually creates **more airflow** over your skin. Those make your body lose heat faster, so it feels cooler. Same reason 65°F with a breeze feels colder than 65°F in still air. And in a car specifically, “65” is often just the requested temperature. The system may still be mixing warm/cool air, compensating for sun through the windshield, and measuring temperature at a sensor location rather than exactly where you’re sitting.
