10:23:09 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That analogy works **if the car cabin is actually at a uniform, steady 65°F**—then yes, how it got there doesn’t matter much. The wrinkle is that a car climate setting is not like an oven thermostat with a sealed, well-mixed chamber. Cars have sun through glass, cold windows, hot dashboards, outside-air leaks, changing vehicle speed, and air blowing directly on occupants. The display’s “65” is usually a **requested target**, while the system continually varies vent temperature, fan speed, recirculation, and compressor use to chase that target. But your core point is right: if both situations truly produce the same air temperature, humidity, airflow, and surface temperatures around you, they should feel the same. AC itself does not create some mysterious separate kind of “65.”
